menu "Atari 8-bit Bus"

config A8BB_ENABLE_MDNS
    bool "Enable mDNS hostname support"
    default n
    help
        Link mDNS support and advertise a MAC-derived hostname on the local
        network. The boot profile must also enable mDNS at runtime.

config A8BB_SYSMONITOR
    bool "System monitor (console-key configuration menu)"
    default n
    help
        The configuration menu the PBI driver shows (main/sysMonitor.cpp),
        entered by holding a console key at boot, the 0xE5 key code, or
        every N seconds (config.sysMonitorSec). Its code and data are in
        internal RAM so it can run while core 1 drives the bus: about 6.5 KB
        of static internal RAM plus its menu items on the internal heap.
        Off: none of that is built, and the monitor never starts.

config A8BB_CORE0_HOTSPOTS_EXPLICIT_PLACEMENT
    bool "Explicitly place Core0 IRAM hotspots"
    default n
    help
        Put the Core0 hotspot functions together and align iloop_pbi to the
        next 32 KiB boundary. This guarantees separation from the Core1 loop,
        at the cost of additional IRAM address space and reduced heap space.
        Not with A8BB_SIDECAR_ENABLE: ldgen orders IRAM code by archive name,
        so libi2slink's hotspots land after the alignment, in the
        Core1 loop's bank, and the post-link check fails the build.

config A8BB_WIFI
    bool "WiFi on main (web server, telnet, SMB connect)"
    default n
    help
        Off in every build for now (2026-11-17, the user: while the
        Sidecar work goes on; the network side moves to the Sidecar).

        Main's own WiFi: the station connect, the web server, telnet and
        mDNS (main/wifi.cpp, telnet.cpp), and wifiPreinit() at boot. Off,
        none of it is reachable, so the linker drops the WiFi driver and
        the PHY blob, whose IRAM (libphy ~5 KB, libpp ~1 KB on the S3) no
        setting moves to flash. The product's network side moves to the
        Sidecar (AGENTS.md TODO: WiFi and local mounts optional on main).

config A8BB_SIDECAR_ENABLE
    bool "Use the Sidecar coprocessor"
    default n
    help
        Have the host use the Sidecar, an optional second ESP32 coprocessor
        (the app in sidecar/), over the i2slink I2S DMA link. The link and
        protocol live in components/sidecar_link and are always compiled;
        this gates the host code that uses them. The link speed is
        I2SLINK_FRAMES_PER_SECOND in i2slink.h and must match on both ends.
        Incompatible with PROFILE_MODE=PIN (core 1 drives GPIO48, the link's
        BCLK); the build stops if both are selected. PROFILE_MODE=GPIO only
        samples pins and works. Also turns off the status LED.

choice A8BB_MAIN_BOARD
    prompt "Main board (address and R/W pin map)"
    default A8BB_BOARD_ESPBUSBANG
    help
        Which main board the firmware drives: the two differ in where R/W
        and A14-A15 sit (main/pinDefs.h).

config A8BB_BOARD_ESPBUSBANG
    bool "espBusBang (the test pool): A0-A15 on GPIO1-16, R/W on GPIO17"

config A8BB_BOARD_CARRIER
    bool "DevKitC carrier / sidecar_pcb_xl: A0-A13 on GPIO1-14, R/W on GPIO15, A14-A15 on GPIO16-17"
    help
        R/W sits right above A8-A13, so core 1 takes the page's table index
        (page in block, R/W) with one extract.
endchoice

config A8BB_REFRESH_GPIO
    int
    default 21
    help
        Where the Atari's refresh_ (active low) reaches main: GPIO21 on
        every board, with or without the Sidecar (its link RX is GPIO18).
        19 (main's USB D-) was the Sidecar builds' while the link RX was
        GPIO21; main's USB-Serial-JTAG pad is then turned off at boot.
        Must be in GPIO_IN_REG (0-31): core 1 tests it in r0. 0: no
        refresh_ (core 1 records refresh cycles, core 0 drops none). No
        prompt (a saved int would keep its old value).

config A8BB_SIDECAR_LOOPBACK
    bool "Loop the Sidecar link back on the host (no Sidecar)"
    depends on A8BB_SIDECAR_ENABLE
    default n
    help
        Test mode: the host's link receives its own transmissions. TX and RX
        share the TX pin (GPIO0), so the Sidecar's RX pin (GPIO21, the Atari
        refresh_ input) is left alone; the host drives BCLK (GPIO48) as it
        always does. Every Sidecar call (probe(), nop(), sio(), cio()) gets
        its own command back as the response, through the normal transact()
        path, and Sidecar::loopbackCheck() also checks the echo byte for
        byte. Runs on a bare host board. A wired Sidecar can stay connected
        (it drives only its own TX wire, which the host then ignores), but
        it still receives and executes the host's commands, so mind
        commands with side effects.

config A8BB_LOG_VIA_SIDECAR
    bool "Send LOG() output to the Sidecar's console"
    depends on A8BB_SIDECAR_ENABLE && !A8BB_SIDECAR_LOOPBACK
    default y
    help
        LOG() lines (components/a8log) also go to the Sidecar, which prints
        them on its console (the SerialOut command). They're queued in a RAM
        ring and sent by the core 0 task, which owns the link: right away
        from cold code, and one frame per PBI request while the bus is hot
        (the 6502 is parked then). Never from the bmon loop's utility pass.
        Needs a Sidecar that says it can (its BootStatus reply); otherwise,
        and until the Sidecar is up, LOG() is printf().

config A8BB_LOG_CONSOLE_TOO
    bool "Print LOG() output on this board's console as well"
    depends on A8BB_LOG_VIA_SIDECAR
    default y
    help
        Keep printing LOG() lines on the host's own console, as printf()
        did. boot.sh, crash bundles and the test pool read that console, so
        this stays on until they read the Sidecar's.

endmenu
